// src/kernel/core/drivers/input/trackpad_route.pdx -- Wave 81 G14 [G3]
//                                                     (paideia-os#2551)
//
// Trackpad gesture -> compositor pan/zoom/swipe routing SKELETON for
// Wave 81 G14 [G3]. Bridges D14 Synaptics gesture decoder output
// (drivers/i2c/synaptics.pdx) into compositor-level pan/zoom/swipe
// events delivered against a focused KIND_SURFACE. D14 emits
// GESTURE_TAP (1) / GESTURE_DOUBLE_TAP (2) / GESTURE_SCROLL (3) /
// GESTURE_SWIPE_3F (4) / GESTURE_PINCH (5); this module maps those
// into compositor-shaped {event_kind, axis, delta_x, delta_y} tuples
// held in a per-focus event queue, then surfaced to a userspace
// compositor via `tp_route_poll_event(out_event_ptr)`.
//
// Sibling parallel-wave landing: Wave 81 G13 keyboard route takes
// KIND_KBD_ROUTE at 0x52; this file takes KIND_TP_ROUTE at 0x53. See
// §8 for the slot audit.
//
// Follows drivers/dpy/damage_plane.pdx (Wave 79 G9, 1041 LOC) as its
// structural template: FROZEN ABI + fingerprint shape at skeleton
// stage; the real pan/zoom/swipe arithmetic (signed scroll deltas,
// 8-point compass direction from (dx, dy), magnification clamp) lands
// in the follow-up slice against the same entrypoints.
//
// =============================================================================
// SECTION 0  SCOPE + HONESTY PIN
// =============================================================================
//
// Posture: EVERY body here is a STUB per the Wave 81 G14 skeleton
// directive. The focused-surface slot, the 16-entry ring queue, the
// gesture->event mapping table, the error taxonomy, and the two
// fingerprint shapes are FROZEN at this skeleton stage so a follow-up
// slice can land the real axis / compass / magnification arithmetic
// against the same ABI without churn. The static state, the
// fingerprint tags, and the counter bumps are real; no live Synaptics
// MMIO is read yet (consume_gesture takes gesture_id/delta_x/delta_y
// as parameters -- the production call site runs
// `synaptics_get_gesture()` from i2c/synaptics.pdx and hands the
// decoded tuple here).
//
// On the Wave 81 G14 skeleton, every entrypoint:
//   (1) range-checks its inputs and refuses with the matching
//       TP_ROUTE_ERR_* sentinel (band 0xFFFFFDFA..0xFFFFFDFE) on the
//       first failing rule;
//   (2) records (or reads) the per-focus state block;
//   (3) bumps the per-op counter so an operator can see the entry
//       on a boot-witness programme;
//   (4) emits a fingerprint on the admitted path.
//
// =============================================================================
// SECTION 1  EVENT KIND DOMAIN  (compositor-visible)
// =============================================================================
//
// `tp_route_poll_event(out_event_ptr)` writes 32 bytes (4 u64s) at
// out_event_ptr laid out as {event_kind, axis, delta_x, delta_y}.
// Event kinds (TP_ROUTE_EK_*):
//
//     0  NONE        -- queue was empty on poll; the other three
//                       fields are 0.
//     1  TAP         -- single-finger click at (delta_x, delta_y).
//                       `axis` is 0.
//     2  DOUBLE_TAP  -- two single-finger clicks within D14's
//                       GESTURE_DOUBLE_TAP window at (delta_x, delta_y).
//                       `axis` is 0.
//     3  SCROLL      -- two-finger pan. `axis` = 0 (horizontal) or 1
//                       (vertical). `delta_x` / `delta_y` are signed
//                       pixel deltas (u64 two's complement -- the
//                       follow-up slice decides whether to narrow to
//                       i32 at the surface-ABI boundary).
//     4  SWIPE_3F    -- three-finger directional swipe. `axis` encodes
//                       the 8-point compass direction, 0 = east,
//                       1 = northeast, 2 = north, ..., 7 = southeast
//                       (counter-clockwise from +X), per the common
//                       Wayland pointer-gesture convention.
//                       `delta_x` / `delta_y` carry the net
//                       displacement for a renderer that wants the
//                       magnitude as well.
//     5  PINCH       -- two-finger pinch/zoom. `delta_x` is the
//                       magnification x 100 (100 = 1.0x, 150 = 1.5x,
//                       50 = 0.5x -- clamp 10..1000 at the gate below).
//                       `axis` is 0. `delta_y` is 0.
//
// The numeric codes 1..5 are deliberately IDENTICAL to Synaptics'
// GESTURE_* domain in `drivers/i2c/synaptics.pdx` §304-309 so the
// production consume_gesture call site can forward the gesture_id
// verbatim. The compositor consumes event_kind, not gesture_id --
// the two names coincide only because D14 happens to be the
// authoritative source today; a future Elan / Touchpad vendor-B
// driver would map ITS gesture codes into the same event_kind
// range at the sibling tp_route_consume_elan boundary.
//
// =============================================================================
// SECTION 2  EVENT QUEUE
// =============================================================================
//
// Per-focus event ring; 16 slots x 32 bytes/slot = 512 bytes =
// 64 u64s. Slot layout mirrors the poll_event output buffer:
//
//     +0   event_kind      -- TP_ROUTE_EK_* (NONE..PINCH)
//     +8   axis            -- SCROLL axis (0/1) OR SWIPE_3F compass (0..7)
//     +16  delta_x         -- signed pixels / magnification x 100
//     +24  delta_y         -- signed pixels
//
// Head / tail cursors are packed into a single u64 `_tp_queue_meta`:
//
//     bits [31:0]   head   -- read cursor (next slot poll will dequeue)
//     bits [63:32]  tail   -- write cursor (next slot push will enqueue)
//
// Both cursors modulo 16 (`and r, 0xF` fits imm8, so the pdx encoder
// handles it directly). Empty: head == tail. Full: ((tail + 1) & 0xF)
// == head. The invariant leaves one slot unused, so the useful
// capacity is 15 pending events; the 16th slot is the full/empty
// discriminator.
//
// Overflow policy: consume_gesture refuses with TP_ROUTE_ERR_QUEUE_
// FULL rather than overwriting the oldest event, same posture as
// damage_plane_commit's NOT_ARMED refusal.
//
// =============================================================================
// SECTION 3  FAILURE TAXONOMY -- 0xFFFFFDFA..0xFFFFFDFE
// =============================================================================
//
// Five codes reserved for this module. Band provenance: the task
// directive originally called for 0xFFFFFE05..09 to stay below the
// crowded 0xFFFFFD prefix; a grep at landing time showed those codes
// are already minted by `cap/kind_ec_query.pdx` (EC_QUERY_REVOKE_
// WRONG_KIND..MINT_BAD_REGION, lines 302-308). The sub-page
// 0xFFFFFDFA..0xFFFFFDFE was free at landing time (grep across
// `src/kernel/` returned ZERO hits on 0xFFFFFDFA / FB / FC / FD / FE),
// so this file takes that band -- immediately below 0xFFFFFDFF
// (claimed by `drivers/i2c/dw_io.pdx` for the STOP_DET mask) and
// above the ec_route.pdx band 0xFFFFFDE0..EF.
//
//     0xFFFFFDFA  TP_ROUTE_ERR_NO_FOCUS       -- no focused surface
//                                                 (focus_set never
//                                                 called, or last
//                                                 focus was cleared
//                                                 by surface_cap=0)
//     0xFFFFFDFB  TP_ROUTE_ERR_BAD_GESTURE    -- gesture_id not in
//                                                 {1,2,3,4,5}
//                                                 (NONE=0 refused as
//                                                 well -- a producer
//                                                 that has no gesture
//                                                 to post should NOT
//                                                 call this entry)
//     0xFFFFFDFC  TP_ROUTE_ERR_QUEUE_FULL     -- 15 events already
//                                                 pending; the next
//                                                 poll must drain at
//                                                 least one before a
//                                                 push admits
//     0xFFFFFDFD  TP_ROUTE_ERR_BAD_EVENT_IDX  -- reserved for a
//                                                 follow-up slice
//                                                 that lets a client
//                                                 random-access the
//                                                 queue by index
//                                                 (TODAY poll_event
//                                                 is strictly FIFO
//                                                 and never emits
//                                                 this code; the
//                                                 reservation makes
//                                                 the band contiguous
//                                                 for a reviewer
//                                                 chasing the taxonomy
//                                                 slice later)
//     0xFFFFFDFE  TP_ROUTE_ERR_BAD_DELTA      -- PINCH magnification
//                                                 outside [10, 1000]
//                                                 (0.1x to 10x). The
//                                                 other gestures
//                                                 impose no delta
//                                                 clamp at skeleton
//                                                 stage; the follow-up
//                                                 slice can tighten
//                                                 (e.g. SCROLL +/- 65k
//                                                 pixels max) against
//                                                 the same code.
//
// =============================================================================
// SECTION 4  VALIDATION RULES (tp_route_consume_gesture)
// =============================================================================
//
// A (gesture_id, delta_x, delta_y) submission is admitted iff:
//
//   (R1) _tp_focused_surface != 0       -> else TP_ROUTE_ERR_NO_FOCUS
//   (R2) gesture_id in {1,2,3,4,5}      -> else TP_ROUTE_ERR_BAD_GESTURE
//   (R3) gesture_id == 5 (PINCH):
//          delta_x in [10, 1000]        -> else TP_ROUTE_ERR_BAD_DELTA
//   (R4) ((tail + 1) & 0xF) != head     -> else TP_ROUTE_ERR_QUEUE_FULL
//
// Rules evaluate in order; the first failing rule's code is returned
// without emitting the EVENT fingerprint. R4 is the LAST gate so a
// bad gesture_id does not spuriously bump a counter for an
// unreachable queue-full case.
//
// On admission the body:
//   (a) computes `axis` from the gesture:
//         TAP        (1)  -> 0
//         DOUBLE_TAP (2)  -> 0
//         SCROLL     (3)  -> |delta_y| > |delta_x| ? 1 : 0
//                            (follow-up slice uses signed abs; at
//                            skeleton stage both deltas are compared
//                            as u64 -- a negative delta's two's
//                            complement value dominates and biases
//                            toward the vertical axis, which is the
//                            desired default when the first slice has
//                            no abs() in hand).
//         SWIPE_3F   (4)  -> delta_x & 0x7  (truncation of a client-
//                            supplied compass index at skeleton stage;
//                            the follow-up slice derives the octant
//                            via atan2(dy, dx) / (pi/4) and clamps
//                            to 0..7 on its own, feeding the result
//                            through delta_x so the ABI holds).
//         PINCH      (5)  -> 0
//   (b) writes (event_kind=gesture_id, axis, delta_x, delta_y) to
//       the tail slot of the ring at _tp_event_queue + tail * 32;
//   (c) advances tail = (tail + 1) & 0xF in _tp_queue_meta;
//   (d) bumps _tp_consume_ct;
//   (e) emits `TP_ROUTE EVENT kind=<dec> axis=<dec>` via klog_s1_d2.
//
// No fingerprint is emitted on gate-failing paths -- the error code
// in rax is the only observable, same posture as damage_plane_submit's
// gate-failing paths.
//
// =============================================================================
// SECTION 5  ENCODER POSTURE (paideia-as v0.36.82)
// =============================================================================
//
//   G1 (sized stores): all state slots are u64 so plain REX.W `mov`
//      is correct for both the queue slot writes and the out_event
//      _ptr fan-out in poll_event. No 32-bit mov_d required at this
//      skeleton stage (poll_event writes u64s per §1's layout).
//   G4 (full-register compares): every cmp here is on a 64-bit
//      register; no `cmp al, imm`, no `cmp eax, imm` short-form.
//   No `test rN, rN` -- equality-against-zero is `cmp rN, 0`.
//   No `and rN, imm64` -- the queue-mask 0xF fits imm8 and the low-
//      byte mask 0xFF fits imm8 too, both encoded directly by the
//      pdx `and r, imm` form. The low-32 / high-32 extractions on
//      _tp_queue_meta use `shl 32; shr 32` (unambiguous) and
//      `shr r, 32`.
//   No two-operand `imul r, imm` -- the per-slot stride is 32,
//      folded as `shl r, 5`.
//   64-bit immediates -- the return-code constants (0xFFFFFDFA..FE)
//      fit imm32 with bit 31 set, so the pdx encoder emits them via
//      the full REX.W `mov r64, imm64` form; proven form across
//      every other FD-band driver (osc.pdx, ec_discovery.pdx, etc.).
//   Multiline `pub let ="..."` is forbidden by the pdx parser; every
//      tag below is a single line.
//   Module basename matches filename basename:
//      trackpad_route.pdx -> module TrackpadRoute.
//   Reserved labels -- every label in this file is prefixed `tp_`
//      so none collides with the dp_/ds_/syn_ labels of the sibling
//      drivers, and none accidentally spells `loop`, `if`, or `let`.
//   klog wrapper discipline -- the two fingerprints each ride an
//      EXISTING wrapper (no new wrappers minted):
//        FOCUS  surface=0x<hex>              -> klog_s1_x1
//        EVENT  kind=<dec> axis=<dec>        -> klog_s1_d2
//      klog_s1_x1 is 5-reg-arg (no stack vd), klog_s1_d2 is 6-reg +
//      caller [rsp+0]=v2. v2 at caller [rsp+0] NOT [rsp+8] -- the
//      klog_s1_d2 caller trap called out in the Wave 81 G14 task
//      directive, which has burned 5+ softarchs across this session.
//   Fingerprint tag length -- each [u8; N] tag's N is strlen + 1
//      (NUL terminator); tag_len off-by-one is a build-time error
//      the pdx encoder catches.
//
// =============================================================================
// SECTION 6  STACK + REGISTER PLAN
// =============================================================================
//
// Four entrypoints:
//
//   tp_route_focus_set       (surface_cap)                 -> u64
//   tp_route_consume_gesture (gesture_id, delta_x, delta_y) -> u64
//   tp_route_poll_event      (out_event_ptr)               -> u64
//   cap_handler_tp_route     (rights, target_ptr, op_arg)  -> u64
//
// All four are non-leaf (nested calls into klog_* and getters).
//
// tp_route_focus_set uses 3 callee-save pushes (rbx = surface_cap).
//   Entry rsp%16==8; after 3 pushes rsp%16==0 (pre-CALL). No vd on
//   stack -- klog_s1_x1 takes all args in regs. No `sub rsp, N`
//   needed before the klog_s1_x1 CALL.
//
// tp_route_consume_gesture uses 5 callee-save pushes (rbx =
//   gesture_id, r12 = delta_x, r13 = delta_y, r14 = computed axis,
//   r15 = scratch for queue cursor + slot ptr). Entry rsp%16==8;
//   after 5 pushes rsp%16==0 (pre-CALL). Then `sub rsp, 16;
//   mov [rsp+0], r14` stages v2=axis for klog_s1_d2 at caller
//   [rsp+0] -- the wrapper reads v2 at [rbp+16] after its own
//   `push rbp`, which is caller [rsp+0] + CALL (-8) + push rbp
//   (-8) = [rbp+16]. Writing v2 at [rsp+8] lands it at [rbp+24]
//   inside the wrapper (the klog_s1_d2 caller trap). `add rsp, 16`
//   reclaims pad + v2 slot.
//
// tp_route_poll_event uses 3 callee-save pushes (rbx = out_event_
//   ptr, r12 = head cursor, r13 = slot ptr). Leaf relative to klog
//   (poll_event does not emit a fingerprint -- a per-poll
//   fingerprint would saturate the wire at the compositor's 60-120 Hz
//   drain rate). The 3 pushes land rsp%16==0; nested calls into
//   getters are leaf, so no further alignment work is required.
//
// cap_handler_tp_route uses 3 callee-save pushes (rbx = rights,
//   r12 = target_ptr/pipe-like, r13 = op_code). klog_s1 entry
//   chatter needs 3 reg args only (no stack vd).
//
// =============================================================================
// SECTION 7  CALLER-SIDE INTEGRATION
// =============================================================================
//
// Production call site (follow-up slice):
//
//     ;; Synaptics ISR path (drivers/i2c/synaptics.pdx):
//     call synaptics_get_gesture      ;; rax = GESTURE_*
//     cmp  rax, 0                     ;; GESTURE_NONE?
//     je   .no_gesture                ;; drop
//     mov  rdi, rax                   ;; gesture_id
//     mov  rsi, _syn_last_dx          ;; delta_x (SCROLL/SWIPE)
//     mov  rdx, _syn_last_dy          ;; delta_y
//     call tp_route_consume_gesture   ;; -> TP_ROUTE_* or 0 (OK)
//
// Compositor drain path (user/compositor):
//
//     sub  rsp, 32                    ;; out_event buffer
//     mov  rdi, rsp
//     call tp_route_poll_event        ;; writes {kind, axis, dx, dy}
//     mov  rax, [rsp + 0]             ;; event_kind
//     cmp  rax, 0                     ;; NONE?
//     je   .no_event                  ;; queue empty, back to vblank
//     ;; dispatch per kind ...
//     add  rsp, 32
//
// The focused-surface slot is written once per focus change by the
// compositor's input-focus manager:
//
//     mov  rdi, <surface_cap>         ;; or 0 to CLEAR focus
//     call tp_route_focus_set         ;; -> 0 (OK)
//
// A zero surface_cap is accepted and clears focus; subsequent
// consume_gesture calls then refuse with TP_ROUTE_ERR_NO_FOCUS until
// a non-zero cap is set again.
//
// =============================================================================
// SECTION 8  WIRING
// =============================================================================
//
// Not wired to the smoke witness at skeleton stage. The file compiles
// alongside the kernel because tools/build.sh recurses through
// src/kernel/core/drivers/ with a single `find`, so a new .pdx
// anywhere under that tree is picked up automatically; no build.sh
// edit required.
//
// invoke.pdx gets ONE new dispatch arm:
//   KIND_TP_ROUTE = 0x53 -> cap_handler_tp_route
//
// Slot audit at landing time (`grep -nE 'cmp rcx, 0x5[2-7]\b'
// `grep -nE 'cmp rcx, 0x53\b' src/kernel/core/cap/invoke.pdx`
// src/kernel/core/cap/invoke.pdx` returned ZERO hits -- 0x52..0x57
// are free. The sibling parallel-wave Wave 81 G13 keyboard route
// takes 0x52; this file takes 0x53. 0x53 fits imm32 bit 31 clear
// (sign-extend-safe) so the arm uses `cmp rcx, 0x53` directly, no
// r9 staging, same posture as every other sub-0x80 kind in the
// R29 pre-block.
//
// No new klog wrapper is minted -- the two fingerprints ride
// klog_s1_x1 (FOCUS) and klog_s1_d2 (EVENT), both pre-existing in
// klog/wrappers.pdx.
//
// No caller invokes tp_route_* yet. A follow-up slice adds a boot-
// witness programme at tests/kernel/drivers/wit_tp_route.pdx that:
//   (1) tp_route_consume_gesture(1, 100, 200) -> TP_ROUTE_ERR_NO_FOCUS;
//   (2) tp_route_focus_set(0xDEAD) -> 0;
//   (3) tp_route_consume_gesture(1, 100, 200) -> 0 (OK);
//   (4) tp_route_poll_event(&out) -> 0 (OK); out.kind == 1;
//   (5) tp_route_poll_event(&out) -> 0; out.kind == 0 (queue drained);
//   (6) tp_route_consume_gesture(5, 5, 0) -> TP_ROUTE_ERR_BAD_DELTA;
//   (7) tp_route_consume_gesture(9, 0, 0) -> TP_ROUTE_ERR_BAD_GESTURE;
// and asserts the FOCUS / EVENT fingerprints land on the klog wire.

module TrackpadRoute = structure {

  // ==========================================================================
  // §A  Event-kind domain (compositor-visible)
  //
  // Public so a boot-witness programme can read-aloud the kind codes
  // rather than hard-code 0..5 at the assert site. Numeric values
  // deliberately IDENTICAL to Synaptics' GESTURE_* domain (see §1 of
  // the file header for the rationale).
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let TP_ROUTE_EK_NONE       : u64 = 0
  pub let TP_ROUTE_EK_TAP        : u64 = 1
  pub let TP_ROUTE_EK_DOUBLE_TAP : u64 = 2
  pub let TP_ROUTE_EK_SCROLL     : u64 = 3
  pub let TP_ROUTE_EK_SWIPE_3F   : u64 = 4
  pub let TP_ROUTE_EK_PINCH      : u64 = 5
  pub let TP_ROUTE_EK_MAX        : u64 = 5

  // ==========================================================================
  // §B  Failure taxonomy -- band 0xFFFFFDFA..0xFFFFFDFE
  //
  // See §3 of the file header for the band provenance (sub-page
  // immediately below the dw_io.pdx STOP_DET mask at 0xFFFFFDFF, above
  // the ec_route.pdx band at 0xFFFFFDE0..EF).
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let TP_ROUTE_OK                    : u64 = 0
  pub let TP_ROUTE_ERR_NO_FOCUS          : u64 = 0xFFFFFDFA
  pub let TP_ROUTE_ERR_BAD_GESTURE       : u64 = 0xFFFFFDFB
  pub let TP_ROUTE_ERR_QUEUE_FULL        : u64 = 0xFFFFFDFC
  pub let TP_ROUTE_ERR_BAD_EVENT_IDX     : u64 = 0xFFFFFDFD
  pub let TP_ROUTE_ERR_BAD_DELTA         : u64 = 0xFFFFFDFE

  // ==========================================================================
  // §C  PINCH magnification bounds (reads with ERR_BAD_DELTA)
  //
  // 10 .. 1000 inclusive on the integer-scaled magnification: 10 =
  // 0.1x (max pinch-in), 1000 = 10x (max pinch-out). Centred at 100
  // = 1.0x (no scale change).
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let TP_ROUTE_PINCH_MIN : u64 = 10
  pub let TP_ROUTE_PINCH_MAX : u64 = 1000

  // ==========================================================================
  // §D  Queue sizing + mask
  //
  // 16 slots x 32 bytes = 512 bytes = 64 u64s. Mask 0xF for the
  // wrap. 15 useful entries (16th slot is the full/empty
  // discriminator per the ring invariant in §2).
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let TP_ROUTE_QUEUE_LEN  : u64 = 16
  pub let TP_ROUTE_QUEUE_MASK : u64 = 15     // 0xF
  pub let TP_ROUTE_SLOT_BYTES : u64 = 32

  // ==========================================================================
  // §E  Static state
  //
  // _tp_focused_surface -- the KIND_SURFACE cap all consume_gesture
  //                        events are routed to. 0 means NO FOCUS;
  //                        consume_gesture refuses until a non-zero
  //                        cap is set.
  // _tp_event_queue     -- 16-slot ring; each slot is 4 u64s laid
  //                        out as {event_kind, axis, delta_x, delta_y}.
  //                        .bss zero-init suffices (all slots start
  //                        at NONE, caller is forbidden to peek the
  //                        ring directly -- poll_event is the only
  //                        exposed reader).
  // _tp_queue_meta      -- packed head|tail cursors
  //                        (bits [31:0]=head, [63:32]=tail).
  //                        .bss zero-init -> head=tail=0 -> empty.
  // _tp_focus_ct        -- bumped on each successful focus_set.
  // _tp_consume_ct      -- bumped on each admitted consume_gesture.
  // _tp_poll_ct         -- bumped on each poll_event call (empty or
  //                        not; a drain-to-empty is still an observed
  //                        poll from the compositor side).
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let mut _tp_focused_surface : u64 = 0
  pub let mut _tp_event_queue : [u64; 64] = uninit @align(64)
  pub let mut _tp_queue_meta  : u64 = 0

  pub let mut _tp_focus_ct   : u64 = 0
  pub let mut _tp_consume_ct : u64 = 0
  pub let mut _tp_poll_ct    : u64 = 0

  // ==========================================================================
  // §F  Fingerprint tags + keys
  //
  // Two tags, one per fingerprinted entrypoint. Each is a single-line
  // string literal; tag_len is strlen + 1 (NUL terminator). poll_event
  // deliberately does NOT fingerprint (saturation at 60-120 Hz
  // compositor-drain rate; see §6).
  //
  // Fingerprints on the wire:
  //   TP_ROUTE FOCUS surface=0x<hex>
  //   TP_ROUTE EVENT kind=<dec> axis=<dec>
  //
  // Matches tests/fingerprints/trackpad_route.golden pattern.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let tp_tag_focus : [u8; 15] = "TP_ROUTE FOCUS\0"
  pub let tp_tag_event : [u8; 15] = "TP_ROUTE EVENT\0"

  pub let tp_k_surface : [u8; 8] = "surface\0"
  pub let tp_k_kind    : [u8; 5] = "kind\0"
  pub let tp_k_axis    : [u8; 5] = "axis\0"

  // Handler-entry chatter tag (for the cap-handler path).
  pub let tp_tag_handler_entry : [u8; 23] = "TP_ROUTE handler entry\0"

  // ==========================================================================
  // §G  tp_route_focus_set(surface_cap) -> u64
  //
  // Set (or clear, when surface_cap == 0) the KIND_SURFACE cap that
  // subsequent consume_gesture events route to. Returns TP_ROUTE_OK.
  // Always admits; a zero cap is a valid "focus cleared" state (the
  // compositor sends it when the last focused window closes).
  //
  // Emits `TP_ROUTE FOCUS surface=0x<hex>` via klog_s1_x1 and bumps
  // _tp_focus_ct.
  //
  // SysV: rdi = surface_cap.
  // Stack recipe: 3 callee-save pushes (rbx = surface_cap).
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let tp_route_focus_set : (u64) -> u64 !{mem, sysreg} @{boot} =
    fn (surface_cap: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem, sysreg}, capabilities: {boot},
      justification: "Wave 81 G14 (paideia-os#2551) skeleton: store surface_cap at _tp_focused_surface + bump _tp_focus_ct + emit `TP_ROUTE FOCUS surface=0x<hex>` via klog_s1_x1. Always admits (TP_ROUTE_OK = 0); a zero surface_cap is a valid 'focus cleared' state and subsequent consume_gesture calls refuse with TP_ROUTE_ERR_NO_FOCUS until a non-zero cap is set again. Register plan: rbx = surface_cap (saved; threaded through to the fingerprint). Three callee-save pushes land rsp%16==0 at the nested klog_s1_x1 CALL (entry %16==8, +24 = 32, %16==0). klog_s1_x1 is 5-reg-arg (level, subsys, tag, k, v) -- no stack vd required, so no `sub rsp, N` before the CALL. The wrapper's own `push rbp; sub rsp, 24` recipe nests cleanly at %16==0. Labels prefixed tp_fs_.",
      block: {
        push rbx;
        push r12;
        push r13;

        mov rbx, rdi;                       // rbx = surface_cap (saved)

        // --- Record state (admitted unconditionally).
        mov [rip + _tp_focused_surface], rbx;

        // --- Count the focus change.
        mov rax, [rip + _tp_focus_ct];
        add rax, 1;
        mov [rip + _tp_focus_ct], rax;

        // --- klog_s1_x1(level=3, SUBSYS_DRV_, tag_focus, k_surface,
        //                surface_cap). All 5 args in registers; no
        //                stack vd needed, no `sub rsp, N` required.
        mov rdi, 3;                         // LEVEL_INFO
        lea rsi, [rip + SUBSYS_DRV_];
        lea rdx, [rip + tp_tag_focus];
        lea rcx, [rip + tp_k_surface];
        mov r8, rbx;                        // v = surface_cap
        call klog_s1_x1;

        xor rax, rax;                       // TP_ROUTE_OK

        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        pop rbx;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // §H  tp_route_consume_gesture(gesture_id, delta_x, delta_y) -> u64
  //
  // Map a D14-shaped (gesture_id, delta_x, delta_y) tuple into a
  // compositor-shaped {event_kind, axis, delta_x, delta_y} event and
  // enqueue it in the ring. Returns TP_ROUTE_OK on admission, else
  // one of the TP_ROUTE_ERR_* sentinels (0xFFFFFDFA..0xFFFFFDFE) on
  // the first failing rule.
  //
  // Gates in order:
  //   (R1) _tp_focused_surface == 0        -> TP_ROUTE_ERR_NO_FOCUS
  //   (R2) gesture_id == 0 || gesture_id > 5  -> TP_ROUTE_ERR_BAD_GESTURE
  //   (R3) gesture_id == 5 (PINCH):
  //          delta_x < 10 || delta_x > 1000 -> TP_ROUTE_ERR_BAD_DELTA
  //   (R4) ring full                       -> TP_ROUTE_ERR_QUEUE_FULL
  //
  // On admission the body computes `axis` per §4(a), writes the
  // 4-u64 slot at _tp_event_queue + tail*32, advances tail with wrap
  // on 0xF, bumps _tp_consume_ct, and emits the EVENT fingerprint.
  //
  // SysV: rdi = gesture_id, rsi = delta_x, rdx = delta_y.
  // Stack recipe: 5 callee-save pushes (rbx = gesture_id, r12 =
  //   delta_x, r13 = delta_y, r14 = computed axis for fingerprint,
  //   r15 = scratch for cursor + slot ptr).
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let tp_route_consume_gesture : (u64, u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem, sysreg} @{boot} =
    fn (gesture_id: u64, delta_x: u64, delta_y: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem, sysreg}, capabilities: {boot},
      justification: "Wave 81 G14 (paideia-os#2551) skeleton: four-gate admission chain over (focus, gesture_id, pinch_delta, queue_depth). Rules in order: (R1) _tp_focused_surface == 0 -> TP_ROUTE_ERR_NO_FOCUS (0xFFFFFDFA); (R2) gesture_id not in {1..5} -> TP_ROUTE_ERR_BAD_GESTURE (0xFFFFFDFB) -- NONE (0) refused because a producer with no gesture should not call this entry at all, and >5 refused as unknown; (R3) PINCH magnification not in [10, 1000] -> TP_ROUTE_ERR_BAD_DELTA (0xFFFFFDFE) -- the follow-up slice can tighten the other gestures' delta clamps against the same code; (R4) ((tail+1) & 0xF) == head -> TP_ROUTE_ERR_QUEUE_FULL (0xFFFFFDFC) -- refuse rather than overwrite the oldest. Admission computes `axis` per the §4 table: TAP/DOUBLE_TAP/PINCH -> 0, SCROLL -> (delta_y > delta_x) ? 1 : 0 as a u64 compare (biases toward vertical when either delta is negative-represented, which is the skeleton default -- the follow-up slice folds in signed abs()), SWIPE_3F -> delta_x & 0x7 as a client-supplied compass passthrough at skeleton stage. Writes a 32-byte slot {event_kind=gesture_id, axis, delta_x, delta_y} at _tp_event_queue + tail*32 (stride via `shl r, 5`); advances tail with wrap on 0xF via a packed u64 load/store on _tp_queue_meta (low-32 = head, high-32 = tail, extracted via `shl 32; shr 32` and `shr r, 32`, repacked via `shl 32; or`). Register plan: rbx = gesture_id (saved; threaded to the slot write and the EVENT fingerprint's `kind=`), r12 = delta_x (saved for slot write + the PINCH delta gate + the SCROLL/SWIPE axis derivation), r13 = delta_y (saved for slot write + the SCROLL axis compare), r14 = computed axis (saved for the fingerprint's `axis=` v2), r15 = scratch for cursor extraction, slot ptr computation, and bump arithmetic. Five callee-save pushes land rsp%16==0 at every nested call (entry %16==8, +40 = 48, %16==0; firmware_blob five-push discipline). klog_s1_d2 caller contract: `sub rsp, 16; mov [rsp+0], r14` writes v2=axis to caller [rsp+0] -- the wrapper reads v2 at [rbp+16] after its own `push rbp`, which is caller [rsp+0] + CALL (-8) + push rbp (-8) = [rbp+16]. Writing v2 at [rsp+8] would land it at [rbp+24] inside the wrapper (the klog_s1_d2 caller trap called out in the Wave 81 G14 task directive, which has burned 5+ softarchs this session). `add rsp, 16` reclaims pad + v2 slot. 0xF, 0xFF, 10, 1000, 5, 32 all fit imm8 or imm16 and the pdx encoder handles them directly; no r9/r10 staging required for any of the gate constants. Labels prefixed tp_cg_.",
      block: {
        push rbx;
        push r12;
        push r13;
        push r14;
        push r15;

        mov rbx, rdi;                       // rbx = gesture_id (saved)
        mov r12, rsi;                       // r12 = delta_x (saved)
        mov r13, rdx;                       // r13 = delta_y (saved)

        // --- Gate (R1): focused surface non-zero.
        mov rax, [rip + _tp_focused_surface];
        cmp rax, 0;
        je  tp_cg_no_focus;

        // --- Gate (R2): gesture_id in {1,2,3,4,5}.
        cmp rbx, 0;
        je  tp_cg_bad_gesture;              // NONE refused
        cmp rbx, 5;
        ja  tp_cg_bad_gesture;              // >5 refused

        // --- Gate (R3): PINCH magnification clamp.
        cmp rbx, 5;                         // gesture_id == PINCH?
        jne tp_cg_delta_ok;
        cmp r12, 10;
        jb  tp_cg_bad_delta;                // delta_x < 10
        cmp r12, 1000;
        ja  tp_cg_bad_delta;                // delta_x > 1000

      tp_cg_delta_ok:
        // --- Gate (R4): queue not full.
        //     Load packed meta: head = low 32, tail = high 32.
        mov r15, [rip + _tp_queue_meta];
        //     Extract head via shl 32; shr 32 (unambiguous mask).
        mov rax, r15;
        shl rax, 32;
        shr rax, 32;                        // rax = head
        //     Extract tail via shr 32.
        mov rcx, r15;
        shr rcx, 32;                        // rcx = tail
        //     Compute (tail + 1) & 0xF.
        mov r9, rcx;
        add r9, 1;
        and r9, 15;                         // 0xF, fits imm8
        //     Full if ((tail+1) & 0xF) == head.
        cmp r9, rax;
        je  tp_cg_queue_full;

        // --- Compute axis per the §4(a) table.
        //     Default axis = 0; override for SCROLL (3) and SWIPE_3F (4).
        xor r14, r14;                       // r14 = 0 by default

        //     SCROLL (3): axis = (delta_y > delta_x) ? 1 : 0.
        cmp rbx, 3;
        jne tp_cg_axis_try_swipe;
        cmp r13, r12;                       // compare delta_y vs delta_x
        jbe tp_cg_axis_done;                // delta_y <= delta_x -> axis=0
        mov r14, 1;                         // vertical
        jmp tp_cg_axis_done;

      tp_cg_axis_try_swipe:
        //     SWIPE_3F (4): axis = delta_x & 0x7 (compass passthrough).
        cmp rbx, 4;
        jne tp_cg_axis_done;
        mov r14, r12;
        and r14, 7;                         // 0..7

      tp_cg_axis_done:
        // --- Compute slot pointer: _tp_event_queue + tail * 32.
        //     rcx still holds tail from the Gate-R4 extraction above.
        lea r15, [rip + _tp_event_queue];
        mov rax, rcx;
        shl rax, 5;                         // tail * 32
        add r15, rax;                       // r15 = slot ptr

        // --- Record event.
        mov [r15 + 0],  rbx;                // event_kind = gesture_id
        mov [r15 + 8],  r14;                // axis
        mov [r15 + 16], r12;                // delta_x
        mov [r15 + 24], r13;                // delta_y

        // --- Advance tail = (tail + 1) & 0xF; repack with head.
        //     Load current meta, extract head preserved from above.
        mov rax, [rip + _tp_queue_meta];
        shl rax, 32;
        shr rax, 32;                        // rax = head (unchanged)
        mov rcx, [rip + _tp_queue_meta];
        shr rcx, 32;                        // rcx = tail
        add rcx, 1;
        and rcx, 15;                        // new tail with wrap
        //     Repack: meta = head | (new_tail << 32).
        shl rcx, 32;
        or  rax, rcx;
        mov [rip + _tp_queue_meta], rax;

        // --- Count the consume.
        mov rax, [rip + _tp_consume_ct];
        add rax, 1;
        mov [rip + _tp_consume_ct], rax;

        // --- klog_s1_d2(level=3, SUBSYS_DRV_, tag_event, k_kind,
        //                gesture_id, k_axis, [v2=axis]).
        //
        // Pre-CALL rsp%16==0 (5 pushes land %16==0; sub rsp,16 keeps
        // %16==0). Wrapper reads v2 at [rbp+16] = caller [rsp+0]
        // after sub rsp,16. v2 at [rsp+0] NOT [rsp+8] -- the
        // klog_s1_d2 caller trap called out in the task directive.
        sub rsp, 16;
        mov [rsp + 0], r14;                 // v2 = axis
        mov rdi, 3;                         // LEVEL_INFO
        lea rsi, [rip + SUBSYS_DRV_];
        lea rdx, [rip + tp_tag_event];
        lea rcx, [rip + tp_k_kind];
        mov r8, rbx;                        // v1 = gesture_id
        lea r9, [rip + tp_k_axis];
        call klog_s1_d2;
        add rsp, 16;

        xor rax, rax;                       // TP_ROUTE_OK
        jmp tp_cg_out;

      tp_cg_no_focus:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFDFA;                // TP_ROUTE_ERR_NO_FOCUS
        jmp tp_cg_out;
      tp_cg_bad_gesture:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFDFB;                // TP_ROUTE_ERR_BAD_GESTURE
        jmp tp_cg_out;
      tp_cg_bad_delta:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFDFE;                // TP_ROUTE_ERR_BAD_DELTA
        jmp tp_cg_out;
      tp_cg_queue_full:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFDFC;                // TP_ROUTE_ERR_QUEUE_FULL

      tp_cg_out:
        pop r15;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        pop rbx;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // §I  tp_route_poll_event(out_event_ptr) -> u64
  //
  // Dequeue the oldest pending event into a caller-owned 32-byte
  // buffer laid out as {event_kind, axis, delta_x, delta_y}, each a
  // u64. Returns TP_ROUTE_OK on both the queue-has-event and the
  // queue-empty paths; the caller distinguishes via the event_kind
  // field (TP_ROUTE_EK_NONE = 0 on empty, 1..5 otherwise).
  //
  // Writes exactly 32 bytes (4 u64s) at out_event_ptr via plain
  // REX.W `mov`. The caller is responsible for the buffer's kernel-
  // visibility; the skeleton does NOT fault-check out_event_ptr.
  // A null out_event_ptr yields a page-fault at the first store,
  // same posture as damage_plane_get_rect and every other getter-
  // into-caller-buffer entry on the drivers branch.
  //
  // No fingerprint -- a per-poll fingerprint saturates the wire at
  // 60-120 Hz compositor drain rate. _tp_poll_ct is bumped
  // unconditionally so a boot-witness programme can observe the
  // call rate.
  //
  // SysV: rdi = out_event_ptr.
  // Stack recipe: 3 callee-save pushes (rbx = out_event_ptr, r12 =
  //   head cursor, r13 = slot ptr).
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let tp_route_poll_event : (u64) -> u64 !{mem, sysreg} @{boot} =
    fn (out_event_ptr: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem, sysreg}, capabilities: {boot},
      justification: "Wave 81 G14 (paideia-os#2551) skeleton: FIFO dequeue of the oldest pending event into a 32-byte caller buffer. If head == tail (queue empty), writes {0,0,0,0} and returns TP_ROUTE_OK -- the caller distinguishes via event_kind == TP_ROUTE_EK_NONE (0). Else: reads 4 u64s at _tp_event_queue + head*32, copies them verbatim to out_event_ptr, advances head = (head + 1) & 0xF with the same packed head|tail meta scheme as consume_gesture. _tp_poll_ct is bumped unconditionally so the compositor's drain rate is observable from a boot-witness programme. No fingerprint -- a 60-120 Hz FOCUS/EVENT-rate fingerprint stream would saturate the klog wire. Register plan: rbx = out_event_ptr (saved; threaded through to the 4 stores), r12 = head cursor (saved for the slot-ptr compute AND the head-bump), r13 = slot ptr. Three callee-save pushes land rsp%16==0 (entry %16==8, +24 = 32, %16==0). No nested CALL on either path (the four stores are plain `mov [rbx + N], rax` and the bump is pure register arithmetic + one static store), so no `sub rsp, N` alignment work is required. out_event_ptr fault-check deferred to the caller (same posture as damage_plane_get_rect, direct_scanout_eligibility). Low-32 / high-32 extraction via `shl 32; shr 32` and `shr r, 32`; repack via `shl 32; or`. Labels prefixed tp_pe_.",
      block: {
        push rbx;
        push r12;
        push r13;

        mov rbx, rdi;                       // rbx = out_event_ptr (saved)

        // --- Count the poll (empty or not; the compositor's drain
        //     rate matters either way).
        mov rax, [rip + _tp_poll_ct];
        add rax, 1;
        mov [rip + _tp_poll_ct], rax;

        // --- Load packed meta; extract head (low 32) + tail (high 32).
        mov rax, [rip + _tp_queue_meta];
        mov r12, rax;
        shl r12, 32;
        shr r12, 32;                        // r12 = head
        mov rcx, rax;
        shr rcx, 32;                        // rcx = tail

        // --- Empty if head == tail -> write {0,0,0,0}, return OK.
        cmp r12, rcx;
        jne tp_pe_dequeue;

        // Empty path.
        xor rax, rax;
        mov [rbx + 0],  rax;                // event_kind = NONE
        mov [rbx + 8],  rax;                // axis
        mov [rbx + 16], rax;                // delta_x
        mov [rbx + 24], rax;                // delta_y
        xor rax, rax;                       // TP_ROUTE_OK
        jmp tp_pe_out;

      tp_pe_dequeue:
        // --- Compute slot ptr: _tp_event_queue + head * 32.
        lea r13, [rip + _tp_event_queue];
        mov rax, r12;
        shl rax, 5;                         // head * 32
        add r13, rax;

        // --- Copy the 4-u64 slot to out_event_ptr.
        mov rax, [r13 + 0];                 // event_kind
        mov [rbx + 0], rax;
        mov rax, [r13 + 8];                 // axis
        mov [rbx + 8], rax;
        mov rax, [r13 + 16];                // delta_x
        mov [rbx + 16], rax;
        mov rax, [r13 + 24];                // delta_y
        mov [rbx + 24], rax;

        // --- Advance head = (head + 1) & 0xF; repack with tail.
        add r12, 1;
        and r12, 15;                        // 0xF
        //     Repack: meta = new_head | (tail << 32).
        //     rcx still holds tail from the extract above.
        shl rcx, 32;
        or  r12, rcx;
        mov [rip + _tp_queue_meta], r12;

        xor rax, rax;                       // TP_ROUTE_OK

      tp_pe_out:
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        pop rbx;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // §J  Focused-surface getter
  //
  // Returns _tp_focused_surface. Leaf; no fingerprint. Public so a
  // boot-witness programme can assert that focus_set stuck.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let tp_route_focused_surface : () -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn () -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "Wave 81 G14 (paideia-os#2551) skeleton: single mov load from _tp_focused_surface. Leaf. Labels prefixed tp_gf_.",
      block: {
        mov rax, [rip + _tp_focused_surface];
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // §K  Queue-depth getter
  //
  // Returns ((tail - head) & 0xF), i.e. the number of pending events
  // waiting on poll (0..15). Leaf; no fingerprint. Public so a boot-
  // witness programme can assert queue depth after a consume + poll
  // without resorting to a raw _tp_queue_meta read.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let tp_route_queue_depth : () -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn () -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "Wave 81 G14 (paideia-os#2551) skeleton: compute (tail - head) & 0xF from _tp_queue_meta. Low-32 / high-32 extraction via shl 32; shr 32 and shr r, 32. 0xF fits imm8. Leaf. Labels prefixed tp_gd_.",
      block: {
        mov rax, [rip + _tp_queue_meta];
        mov rcx, rax;
        shl rcx, 32;
        shr rcx, 32;                        // rcx = head
        shr rax, 32;                        // rax = tail
        sub rax, rcx;
        and rax, 15;                        // depth mod 16
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // §L  Per-op counter getters
  //
  // Three leaf getters so a boot-witness programme can assert
  // (focus_ct, consume_ct, poll_ct) without touching the _tp_*_ct
  // symbols directly.
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let tp_route_focus_ct : () -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn () -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "Wave 81 G14 (paideia-os#2551) skeleton: single mov load from _tp_focus_ct. Leaf.",
      block: {
        mov rax, [rip + _tp_focus_ct];
        ret
      }
    }

  pub let tp_route_consume_ct : () -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn () -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "Wave 81 G14 (paideia-os#2551) skeleton: single mov load from _tp_consume_ct. Leaf.",
      block: {
        mov rax, [rip + _tp_consume_ct];
        ret
      }
    }

  pub let tp_route_poll_ct : () -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn () -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "Wave 81 G14 (paideia-os#2551) skeleton: single mov load from _tp_poll_ct. Leaf.",
      block: {
        mov rax, [rip + _tp_poll_ct];
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // §M  cap_handler_tp_route(rights, target_ptr, op_arg) -> u64
  //
  // KIND_TP_ROUTE (0x53) cap_invoke dispatch handler. Reachable from
  // cap/invoke.pdx:cap_invoke_dispatch via the `cmp rcx, 0x53;
  // je call_kind_tp_route` arm added alongside this file.
  //
  // The three main entrypoints each take more than three arguments
  // (consume_gesture: 3, poll_event: 1 but one is an out-pointer,
  // focus_set: 1), so cap_invoke -- which hands the handler only
  // (rights, target_ptr, op_arg) -- cannot plumb consume_gesture
  // directly without a packed op_arg or an extended signature. The
  // handler is therefore QUERY-ONLY at skeleton stage, exactly like
  // damage_plane / direct_scanout / iris_xe_hotplug do for their
  // first slice. Five ops:
  //
  //     op 0 TP_ROUTE_OP_QUERY_FOCUS       -> _tp_focused_surface
  //     op 1 TP_ROUTE_OP_QUERY_FOCUS_CT    -> _tp_focus_ct
  //     op 2 TP_ROUTE_OP_QUERY_CONSUME_CT  -> _tp_consume_ct
  //     op 3 TP_ROUTE_OP_QUERY_POLL_CT     -> _tp_poll_ct
  //     op 4 TP_ROUTE_OP_QUERY_DEPTH       -> (tail - head) & 0xF
  //     else                                   INVOKE_UNSUPPORTED
  //
  // The mutating primitives (focus_set / consume_gesture / poll_
  // event) ride supervisor callers above the generic cap_invoke
  // syscall ABI; a follow-up slice extends cap_invoke with a 4-
  // register slot variant OR packs the args into target_ptr/op_arg.
  //
  // Rights gate: every op requires R_TP_ROUTE_READ (0x001).
  //
  // SysV: rdi = rights, rsi = target_ptr, rdx = op_arg.
  // Stack recipe: 3 callee-save pushes (rbx = rights, r12 =
  //   target_ptr, r13 = op_code).
  // ==========================================================================

  pub let R_TP_ROUTE_READ : u64 = 0x001

  pub let TP_ROUTE_OP_QUERY_FOCUS      : u64 = 0
  pub let TP_ROUTE_OP_QUERY_FOCUS_CT   : u64 = 1
  pub let TP_ROUTE_OP_QUERY_CONSUME_CT : u64 = 2
  pub let TP_ROUTE_OP_QUERY_POLL_CT    : u64 = 3
  pub let TP_ROUTE_OP_QUERY_DEPTH      : u64 = 4

  pub let cap_handler_tp_route : (u64, u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem, sysreg} @{cap} =
    fn (rights: u64, target_ptr: u64, op_arg: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem, sysreg}, capabilities: {cap},
      justification: "Wave 81 G14 (paideia-os#2551) skeleton: KIND_TP_ROUTE (0x53) cap_invoke dispatch. Entry chatter (klog_s1 of tp_tag_handler_entry). op_code = op_arg & 0xFF; rights gate against R_TP_ROUTE_READ (single up-front check); dispatch 0..4 to the matching reader (tp_route_focused_surface / tp_route_focus_ct / tp_route_consume_ct / tp_route_poll_ct / tp_route_queue_depth); else INVOKE_UNSUPPORTED. target_ptr unused at skeleton stage (reserved for a future indexed-op variant that reads op 5 TP_ROUTE_OP_QUERY_EVENT_AT with target_ptr as the slot index, backed by TP_ROUTE_ERR_BAD_EVENT_IDX 0xFFFFFDFD). Three callee-save pushes land rsp%16==0 at every nested call (entry %16==8, +24 = 32, %16==0). The mutating ops (focus_set / consume_gesture / poll_event -- all take >1 arg or an out-pointer) are deferred to a follow-up slice that extends cap_invoke with a 4-register slot variant OR packs the args into target_ptr/op_arg. 0xFF fits imm8; 0..4 fit imm8; no r9/r10 staging required. Labels prefixed tp_hd_.",
      block: {
        push rbx;
        push r12;
        push r13;

        mov rbx, rdi;                       // rights
        mov r12, rsi;                       // target_ptr (reserved)
        mov r13, rdx;                       // op_arg
        and r13, 255;                       // op_code = op_arg[7:0]; 0xFF fits imm8

        // Entry chatter.
        mov rdi, 3;                         // LEVEL_INFO
        lea rsi, [rip + SUBSYS_DRV_];
        lea rdx, [rip + tp_tag_handler_entry];
        call klog_s1;

        // Rights gate.
        mov rax, rbx;
        and rax, 1;                         // R_TP_ROUTE_READ (0x001); imm8
        cmp rax, 0;
        je  tp_hd_denied;

        // Op dispatch.
        cmp r13, 0;
        je  tp_hd_op_focus;
        cmp r13, 1;
        je  tp_hd_op_focus_ct;
        cmp r13, 2;
        je  tp_hd_op_consume_ct;
        cmp r13, 3;
        je  tp_hd_op_poll_ct;
        cmp r13, 4;
        je  tp_hd_op_depth;
        jmp tp_hd_unsup;

      tp_hd_op_focus:
        call tp_route_focused_surface;
        jmp tp_hd_out;

      tp_hd_op_focus_ct:
        call tp_route_focus_ct;
        jmp tp_hd_out;

      tp_hd_op_consume_ct:
        call tp_route_consume_ct;
        jmp tp_hd_out;

      tp_hd_op_poll_ct:
        call tp_route_poll_ct;
        jmp tp_hd_out;

      tp_hd_op_depth:
        call tp_route_queue_depth;
        jmp tp_hd_out;

      tp_hd_denied:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFD;        // INVOKE_DENIED
        jmp tp_hd_out;
      tp_hd_unsup:
        mov rax, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFC;        // INVOKE_UNSUPPORTED

      tp_hd_out:
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        pop rbx;
        ret
      }
    }

  // ==========================================================================
  // §N  Boot wiring note
  // ----------------
  // kernel_main.pdx does NOT need a dedicated reset entry: .bss zero-
  // init leaves _tp_focused_surface = 0 (no focus), _tp_queue_meta =
  // 0 (head == tail == 0, queue empty), the entire _tp_event_queue
  // ring at zero, and the three counters at zero -- which is exactly
  // the never-focused never-posted starting state the consume / poll
  // paths expect. A follow-up slice may mint a defensive
  // `tp_route_reset` for parity with the other input drivers'
  // reset entries, but the skeleton gets by on .bss alone.
  //
  // invoke.pdx picks up the KIND_TP_ROUTE (0x53) dispatch arm in the
  // same slice as this file -- the arm is pure plumbing and self-
  // standing (handler returns INVOKE_UNSUPPORTED for every mutating
  // op at skeleton stage, which is observable but harmless).
  //
  // No new klog wrapper is minted; the two fingerprints ride
  // klog_s1_x1 (FOCUS) and klog_s1_d2 (EVENT), both pre-existing in
  // klog/wrappers.pdx.
  //
  // Follow-up slices (G14+):
  //   - Real axis derivation for SCROLL (signed abs over delta_x /
  //     delta_y rather than the u64 compare at skeleton stage).
  //   - Real 8-point compass for SWIPE_3F (atan2(dy, dx) / (pi/4)
  //     over fixed-point in the kernel's math/fixed_point.pdx).
  //   - Delta clamps for SCROLL / SWIPE_3F against the same ERR_BAD_
  //     DELTA code (follow-up slice ships them in that order so the
  //     error-band stays contiguous).
  //   - cap_handler op 5 TP_ROUTE_OP_QUERY_EVENT_AT with target_ptr
  //     as the slot index (backed by TP_ROUTE_ERR_BAD_EVENT_IDX
  //     0xFFFFFDFD, which is minted here but not yet observable).
  //   - cap_handler extension for the three mutating ops
  //     (packed target_ptr + op_arg for consume_gesture, OR a
  //     4-register cap_invoke slot variant that fits the full
  //     (gesture_id, delta_x, delta_y) tuple).
  //   - Production call site in drivers/i2c/synaptics.pdx post-decode,
  //     calling tp_route_consume_gesture with
  //     synaptics_get_gesture() + the resolved deltas.
  // ==========================================================================
}
